{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit transfer and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices, paying interest and returning it; willfully withholding deposits of tenants on public assistance carries a civil penalty.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies in New Jersey; the $500-$2,000 penalty applies where the tenant has received State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"The withholding penalty applies where the deposit was made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense for willful withholding, enforced by summary proceedings; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200, up to 30 days imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey has no statewide rent control; municipal rent control authority","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental units in municipalities that have adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; local boards enforce those ordinances.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"unit is covered only if its municipality has adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling."]},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may be exempt from local rent control; HUD-owned or HUD-subsidized housing and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Municipal rent control ordinances (N.J. Dept. of Community Affairs, Truth in Renting)","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":"Municipal rent control ordinances are enforced by locally created boards.","relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction, and rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; exempt from local rent control for 30 years after construction is completed. Subsidized rents may also be exempt.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built_within_years","role":"exempt","years":30,"basis":"construction"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building"]},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years after completion; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts newly constructed multiple dwellings from local rent control ordinances.","relations":[{"type":"preempts_local","quote":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building"}],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability limit","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered with proper written notice, and the increase may not be unconscionable or violate municipal rent control.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rentals; rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a landlord seeks to raise rent: the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered; an unconscionable increase may be challenged.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A tenant may complain to a municipal rent control board where one exists; where no municipal rent control exists, a judge decides in an eviction action whether the increase is unconscionable.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"The increase must comply with municipal rent control ordinances where they exist; the tenant may complain to a municipal rent control board.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on rent increases for condominium or cooperative conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium or cooperative ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, regardless of eligibility for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["This prohibition applies to all tenants in the building regardless of whether they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons."]},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause and notice requirements","requirement":"A landlord may evict or refuse to renew a residential lease only for good cause, stated in a written Notice to Quit (except for nonpayment of rent), and must obtain a court judgment for possession.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as residences; no residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination protection for source of lawful income","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in housing because of a renter's source of lawful income, and the LAD requires equal treatment on the other grounds it lists.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied two-family dwelling","unit_limit":2},{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner- or resident-occupied single home (rooms)","unit_limit":null}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law applies to all landlord -tenant relationships, except those involving two -family owner occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner or resident -occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, i.e. YMCA and age -restricted housing, as it pertains to familial status (N.J.S.A. 10:5-5(n))."]},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned for and occupied by one sex, including YMCA and age-restricted housing as to familial status.","penalty":"Under the LAD, violators face fines of up to $10,000 for a first offense, and may have to pay monetary damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
